hi I'm fair and cousins sitting in for  David Pakman and this is the david  pakman show thanks for joining us listen  I know a lot of people have been very  anxious recently wondering about the  outcome of the war on poverty after all  this is a war that we have been fighting  as a country since the early 1960s well  I am proud to announce that according to  the Trump administration we have finally  won the war on poverty all our poverty  troops can come home rejoin the families  that they've been missing for decades  because the war on poverty is officially  over and we won poverty lost now  obviously that's not true right we all  know that poverty still exists in a very  big way here in the United States but  that didn't stop this administration  recently from telling the world that we  beat poverty here in the United States  and they did that based on faulty math  from the administration what happened  was that they claimed that since 1961  the poverty rate in the United States  has fallen from 30 percent to 3 percent  now 3 percent poverty would be a  phenomenal measure of in any country  anybody would be happy to only have 3  percent poverty unfortunately again  they're using the wrong map what the  administration did to figure that 3  percent number was that they based their  poverty measure on purchases purchasing  power of American citizens which happens  to include buying things with food  stamps or getting medical care with a  program like Medicaid that's money that  comes from the federal government but  you spent it you spent it therefore you  have greater purchasing power therefore  you are not in poverty reality says that  the poverty rate in the United States is  somewhere between 12 point 7 percent  according to the government and 14  percent according to the US Census  Bureau based on the proper measure of  poverty  we  which is a person's income how much  money they get each month so anywhere  between twelve point seven to fourteen  percent is what the real number is it is  down from what we saw you know during  the Bush administration those numbers  hit almost 18 percent at that time but  it's still a very real problem  so why in god's name would this  administration be telling us that we won  the war on poverty well they actually  answered that question in their own  little announcement about winning the  war it's because they want to cut social  safety net programs so they have to make  it seem like those programs are  unnecessary here in the United States  one of the big drivers of this let's cut  everything in the world that helps the  less fortunate is Paul Ryan and as we  all know Paul Ryan not seeking  re-election he's only got a few more  months in office but it has been a dream  of his since he was a child to cut the  social safety net out of the United  States of America I mean most little  kids go outside they play they pretend  to be superheroes Paul Ryan went outside  and played and I'm assuming practiced  with a pair of scissors pretending he  was cutting that safety net cutting red  tape that was his superpower getting rid  of protections that keep American  citizens alive and that's what he wants  to do here and he's using these faulty  poverty numbers to do it and the  administration right now is enabling him  if you think back a few weeks ago the UN  did a report on what they call extreme  poverty here in the United States those  are people who are living on basically  less than about $4 per day extreme  poverty they estimated that it's about  18 million American citizens living  under those conditions the  administration snapped back at them and  said nope  by our count which we don't let you see  the numbers it's only about 250,000 that  that live under those conditions which  means that it doesn't exist they're  doing the same thing here with just  regular poverty numbers we know it  exists  depending on where you live in this  country it's not that hard to see it is  an everyday reality for millions upon  millions of American citizens take it  from me listen I live in the south we  see it all around us most of us at one  point of our lives or maybe even right  now are in it it's not pleasant it's not  pretty but it is very very real and for  this administration to try to use this  one to lie and say that it's not  happening but to to cut away the safety  net that is absolutely deplorable and I  think that is an apt descriptor for this  deplorable here's the thing Paul Ryan  doesn't think we won the war on poverty  he doesn't think that poverty has gone  away in the United States but he does  still think that because things are  decent in the economy at this very  second we should cut the social safety  nets a couple weeks ago Paul Ryan had  this to say in a speech he said in this  economy with all of the opportunities  available there could not be a better  time to help more people move from  welfare to work so he is out there still  today this week last week on his Twitter  feed telling us that things are so great  that all of you lazy people you can go  find a job because in Republican world  if you're on welfare it is 100% because  you're because you're lazy  not maybe because you had an accident  you're disabled not maybe because the  job you had been working for 25 years  got shipped overseas because companies  wanted to save a few dollars here and  there not maybe because of the trade  wars that are currently costing hundreds  upon hundreds of jobs every week in this  country no you're lazy in Paul Ryan's  eyes and in the eyes of too many  Republican leaders in Washington DC the  social safety net keeps people from  living on the streets and even then  sometimes it can't completely keep  people off that there are work  requirements for  most of these programs but Republicans  would rather go out there and repeat the  lies that they have been telling us  since the 1980s about these alleged  welfare queens the people live in high  on the hog off the government's a dollar  which could not be further from the  truth they would rather lie to you about  that than admit the reality that maybe  this economy is not perfect maybe the  private sector is not helping the  situation by sending jobs over the seas  maybe just maybe the federal government  needs to help these people because if  they had to admit that that would  destroy the entire mythos that they have  created as a political party since again  at least that early 1980s possibly going  as far back as the New Deal when  Republicans obviously didn't think the  government should be offering any of  these programs we have a problem here in  the United States of very extreme  poverty and just because things are  chugging along at the moment with the  economy every credible economist out  there is telling us things are about to  come crashing down what we have is an  economy built out of straw something  that is not sustainable something that  is exceptionally fragile and when you  combine things like rising interest  rates which are happening right now  trade wars which are happening right now  income inequality getting greater which  is happening right now  any one of those things has the  potential to devastate a fragile economy  we have all of them so Paul Ryan can  boast all he wants on Twitter right now  about how great the economy is Trump was  doing it this morning as well but at the  end of the day they know the truth they  know that poverty is real they know that  the economy is fragile and they know  that their policies both Trump and Ryan  those two guys that are pushing for this  their policies are going to be the ones  that bring everything crashing down
 

